WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.660
Somewhere in your code,
something is failing right now.

2
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:04.840
It won't crash.

3
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.360
It will fail quietly,
somewhere nobody is looking.

4
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.640
So here's a question
almost nobody can answer.

5
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.220
When it fails, who hears about it?

6
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.000
This is Faultline, by EKG.

7
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.240
It reads a codebase and draws
what it does, on one map.

8
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.540
Every decision. Every
wait. Every way it can end.

9
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.160
The map you're looking at is Faultline's
own code, drawn by Faultline.

10
00:00:29.924 --> 00:00:31.384
No model draws it.

11
00:00:32.224 --> 00:00:35.764
Programs read the code, a type
checker settles who calls whom,

12
00:00:35.944 --> 00:00:38.284
and plain rules turn each decision,

13
00:00:38.764 --> 00:00:41.164
loop and ending into boxes and roads.

14
00:00:41.944 --> 00:00:45.204
Run it twice on the same commit,
and you get the same bytes.

15
00:00:46.086 --> 00:00:49.426
Geologists draw a fault solid
where they know where it is,

16
00:00:49.506 --> 00:00:51.546
and dashed where they're inferring it.

17
00:00:52.246 --> 00:00:53.446
Faultline does the same.

18
00:00:54.406 --> 00:00:55.886
Solid where the code proves it.

19
00:00:56.426 --> 00:01:00.426
Dashed, with a question mark, where it
doesn't, and the panel tells you why.

20
00:01:01.212 --> 00:01:03.552
Go from the whole system, to one stage,

21
00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:06.232
to one function, to one line of code,

22
00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:07.552
without losing your place.

23
00:01:08.396 --> 00:01:12.156
One map, two readers. People
get one page to explore.

24
00:01:12.916 --> 00:01:17.676
Agents get the same map as short text,
with a token cap on every answer.

25
00:01:18.496 --> 00:01:23.296
Same box, same id, so you can hand
your agent the exact spot you mean.

26
00:01:24.045 --> 00:01:27.565
At every failure, it asks
who finds out. A person?

27
00:01:27.885 --> 00:01:31.125
The calling code? A log? Or nobody at all?

28
00:01:31.425 --> 00:01:34.685
When the code doesn't say, the
map says so, instead of guessing.

29
00:01:35.592 --> 00:01:37.612
An agent can't draw on the map.

30
00:01:38.232 --> 00:01:40.772
It can answer the
questions the tool flagged,

31
00:01:41.052 --> 00:01:45.132
and write the story on top, and
every word stays marked as words.

32
00:01:46.044 --> 00:01:51.264
We tried it on BullMQ, an open source
job queue of thirty thousand lines,

33
00:01:51.424 --> 00:01:56.044
both ways: programs alone, and
with an agent's story on top.

34
00:01:56.804 --> 00:01:59.244
It's all on the site,
including what didn't work.

35
00:02:00.026 --> 00:02:02.766
Faultline, by EKG. Free and open source.

36
00:02:03.326 --> 00:02:04.406
Point it at your own code.
